course: world
lesson: 14
title: The water cycle
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The water on Earth is used again and again in a never-ending journey called the water cycle.
explain: The Sun warms seas, lakes, rivers and soil, and water evaporates into the air as invisible vapour; plants also give off water vapour from their leaves.
explain: As the moist air rises it cools, and the vapour condenses on tiny specks of dust, smoke and sea salt into droplets that make clouds.
explain: When the droplets join together and grow heavy, they fall as rain, snow or hail: this is called precipitation.
explain: The water runs over the land in streams and rivers or soaks into the ground, flows back to the sea; this gathering of water back into rivers, lakes and seas is called collection, and the journey starts again.
explain: For example, a raindrop that lands on you today may once have been in an ocean, in a cloud over another continent, or even drunk by a dinosaur.
fact: The heat of the Sun drives the water cycle.
fact: Evaporation lifts water from seas, lakes and soil into the air.
fact: Plants give off water vapour from their leaves; this is called transpiration.
fact: Air cools as it rises, so water vapour condenses high up into clouds.
fact: A cloud is made of millions of tiny water drops, or of tiny ice crystals where it is very cold.
fact: Rain, snow, sleet and hail are all called precipitation.
fact: Some rainwater soaks into the ground and becomes groundwater, which feeds wells and springs.
fact: When seawater evaporates the salt stays behind, so rain is fresh water.
fact: Most of Earth's water is salty water in the oceans; only a small part is fresh.
fact: Much of Earth's fresh water is frozen in ice sheets, huge sheets of ice covering land, and glaciers, slow rivers of ice.
fact: The amount of water on Earth stays about the same; it just keeps moving around.
exercise: What gives the water cycle its energy? | The heat of the Sun | The pull of the Moon | The wind on its own | Heat from deep inside the Earth
exercise: What happens to water vapour as it rises high into the air? | It cools and condenses into cloud droplets | It gets hotter and hotter until it disappears | It freezes the air around it | It turns into oxygen
exercise: What is precipitation? | Water falling from clouds as rain, snow or hail | Water rising up from the sea into the sky as vapour | Water soaking slowly into the soil | Water flowing along in rivers
exercise: Why is rain fresh when much of it comes from the salty sea? | The salt stays behind when seawater evaporates | The clouds filter the salt out | The salt melts away inside the clouds | The wind blows the salt off the raindrops as they fall
exercise: How do plants take part in the water cycle? | They give off water vapour from their leaves | They make brand new water out of the soil they grow in | They stop the rain from falling | They turn water into air
exercise: Where can rain go after it soaks into the ground? | It becomes groundwater that feeds springs and wells | It disappears for ever | It slowly turns into rock | It goes straight back up to the clouds the very next day
exercise: Is new water being made all the time on Earth? | No, the same water keeps going round the cycle | Yes, the clouds keep making brand new water all the time | Yes, the rivers keep making new water as they flow | Yes, the Sun keeps making new water every single day
exercise: Why do clouds usually form high above the ground? | Air cools as it rises, so the vapour condenses up there | Clouds are lighter than outer space | The wind lifts up clouds that first form on the ground below | The Sun pulls the clouds up towards it
exercise: What do cloud droplets form on? | Tiny specks of dust, smoke and salt in the air | Nothing, they form in perfectly clean air, high in the sky | The feathers of birds flying past | Only ash thrown up by volcanoes
exercise: Which order is correct? | Evaporation, condensation, precipitation, collection | Condensation, evaporation, collection, precipitation | Precipitation, evaporation, condensation, melting | Collection, precipitation, evaporation, freezing
exercise: Where is most of Earth's water? | In the salty oceans | In rivers and lakes | In the clouds | Deep under the ground
exercise: Water evaporates from a big lake every summer day. Why does the lake usually not dry up? | Rivers, springs and rain bring new water into it | Evaporation stops in summer | Lakes are far too deep to lose any water at all in summer | The fish in it make more water
exercise: Where is much of Earth's fresh water stored? | Frozen in ice sheets and glaciers | High up in the clouds above us all | In the salty water of the great oceans | In puddles, ponds and little streams
exercise: What is a cloud made of? | Millions of tiny water drops or ice crystals | Only invisible water vapour | Smoke and soot from fires | Soft dust like cotton wool
test: A raindrop falls into a river. What could happen to it next? | It flows to the sea, evaporates and falls as rain again | It stays in that river for ever | It slowly turns into salt | It vanishes, and brand new water is made somewhere else instead
test: Which part of the water cycle happens on a cold window when you breathe on it? | Condensation | Precipitation | Evaporation | Transpiration
test: If the Sun stopped warming the Earth, what would happen to the water cycle? | It would stop, because water would hardly evaporate | Rain would fall more often | Clouds would form even faster than before, and rain more | Nothing, because the Moon drives it
test: Which is true about rain? | It is fresh water, even when it came from the sea | It is salty, because it all came from the sea in the first place | It is made brand new inside clouds | It comes only from rivers and lakes
test: A large forest is cut down. How might this change the water cycle there? | Less water vapour goes into the air from leaves | The rain that falls there becomes salty | More water vapour starts coming out of the cut stumps | The rivers there start to flow uphill
test: You tie a clear plastic bag over a leafy branch on a sunny day. Later there are drops of water inside. Why? | The leaves gave off vapour that condensed on the bag | Rain got in through the plastic | The plastic made the water itself | Dew from the morning soaked through the bag
test: Which word names hail falling from a storm cloud? | Precipitation | Evaporation | Condensation | Transpiration
test: Why could the water you drink today have been in a cloud long ago? | Earth's water goes round and round the cycle | Water only lasts for one day | Clouds are made of new water each day | Tap water is made new in a factory
test: Snow on a mountain melts in spring. Where does the water go? | Into streams and rivers, and on towards the sea | Straight up into outer space | Into the rock, where it stays for ever | It turns straight back into snow
test: where does rain even come from | Water that evaporated, rose and condensed into clouds | Water that clouds pull up from deep underground | New water the sky makes every day | Water that falls off the Moon
test: how come rain isn't salty if it comes from the sea | The salt stays behind when seawater evaporates | The clouds filter the salt out | The rain is salty, we just can't taste it | The wind blows the salt away
test: my brother says we'll run out of water one day cos it all gets used up. true? | No, the water on Earth keeps moving round and stays about the same | Yes, every glass we drink is gone for ever | Yes, the Sun burns some water away every year | No, the Earth makes new water every day
test: Mia says most of Earth's water is fresh water from rivers. right? | No, most is salty seawater in the oceans | Yes, rivers hold most of the water | Yes, and the oceans are just a small part | No, most is frozen in the clouds
exam: Identify the principal source of energy by which the water cycle is driven. | The heat of the Sun | The gravitational pull of the Moon | The heat of the Earth's interior | The force of the wind alone
exam: Which of the following is designated by the term transpiration? | The emission of water vapour from the leaves of plants | The descent of rain upon the land | The absorption of rainwater into the ground | The freezing of water within glaciers
exam: Identify the principal reason for which rain is fresh water even when derived from the sea. | The salt remains behind upon evaporation | The clouds filter out the salt as they form | The salt is consumed entirely by the Sun's heat | The wind removes the salt from the falling rain
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the total quantity of water upon the Earth. | It remains approximately constant | It increases by a small amount each year | It diminishes by a small amount each year | It is entirely renewed upon an annual basis
